 ### Fixed
 
+- `onError` now fires for every rejection inside a turn, not only for rejections carrying an `Error`. A provider (or a hook inside the `try`) that throws a string, a plain object or `undefined` used to bypass `onError` entirely while the rejection still reached the caller, so a hook counting failures or paging on them saw a clean turn for exactly the failures it existed to report. Such a value now reaches the hook wrapped in an `Error` with a readable `message` and the original preserved as `cause`, so a hook typed against `Error` keeps compiling and one that wants the raw value can still reach it. The rejection the caller receives is unchanged — the original value is re-thrown exactly as before. **Migration note**: an `onError` hook that records incidents will now also record these rejections, so failure counts can rise for turns that previously looked clean; that is the undercount being corrected.
 - `onError` and `afterSession` hooks now fire when `beforeSession` throws, matching the guarantee the hook names imply. Previously, a throw from `beforeSession` escaped the `try`/`finally` block entirely, so neither hook ran. **Migration note**: if your `afterSession` hook assumed it would only be called after a full session turn, it will now also be called when `beforeSession` itself fails — the hook can inspect the session to detect this case (no entries beyond the initial user message will have been processed).
 
